From 3f0034e99a45ed2b800980fe495df8ce67dfe15f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nex <63727521+LuCiLuna@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 21:44:45 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 109/110] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8350: scale DDR, LLCC and L3 with
 demand

Nothing voted for memory on behalf of the CPUs or the GPU: only display,
video, PCIe and UFS held DDR votes, which kept DDR slow however busy the
system was. memcpy measured 3-4 GB/s with 240-280 ns latency.
Playing 4K60 VP9 in a browser starved the video block on top of that.

Add what the vendor tree has, using its frequency tables and the wiring
sc7280 already uses upstream, since the monitors are the same generation
at the same addresses:

 - BWMON v4 for CPU to LLCC traffic and BWMON v5 for LLCC to DDR, which
   also sees GPU and video traffic
 - CPU OPP tables that vote DDR and L3 by frequency, and the EPSS L3 node
 - a GPU memory path with peak bandwidth in each OPP

memcpy now reaches 17 GB/s at 142 ns on the prime core, with DDR scaled
automatically.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
